# {{ workload.project }}: k6 performance test scripts

Generated by perfagent ({{ generated }}). **Self-contained:** these scripts run with only the [k6](https://grafana.com/docs/k6/latest/set-up/install-k6/) binary. You don't need Python, the perfagent framework, an AI assistant or internet access. You can copy this folder anywhere (another machine, a CI agent, a load-generator VM).

## Run
```bash
k6 run main.js \
  -e TEST_TYPE=smoke \
  -e BASE_URL={{ base_url }} \
  -e WEB_URL={{ web_url }}{% for v in env_vars %} \
  -e {{ v }}=<secret>{% endfor %}

```
PowerShell: put the whole command on one line, or use a backtick (`` ` ``) for line continuation.

| Variable | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `TEST_TYPE` | {% for name in workload.profiles %}`{{ name }}`{{ " · " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %} (load shapes in `workload.json` → `profiles`) |
| `BASE_URL` | API base URL the scripts call |
| `WEB_URL` | Front-end URL for browser scenarios |
| `SCALE` | Optional load multiplier, e.g. `0.5` (default `1`) |
| `SUMMARY_PATH` | Where to write the full JSON results (default `summary.json` in the current folder) |
{% for v in env_vars %}
| `{{ v }}` | Secret used by the scenarios. Pass it at runtime; never write it into these files |
{% endfor %}

Optional extras:
- `K6_WEB_DASHBOARD=true K6_WEB_DASHBOARD_EXPORT=dashboard.html`: live dashboard at http://localhost:5665 and an HTML export. Close the tab when the test ends.
- `--out influxdb=http://localhost:8086/k6`: stream metrics to Grafana.
- `K6_BROWSER_HEADLESS=false`: watch the browser scenarios.

## Results
- The console prints a PASS/FAIL table per scenario against the SLAs.
- **Exit code 0** means all SLAs were met. **99** means a threshold (SLA) was breached. Other non-zero codes are errors. CI pipelines can gate on this.
- `summary.json` holds the complete metrics. The perfagent framework turns it into an HTML report (`python -m perfagent report`), but that's optional.

## Contents
| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `main.js` | Entry point: options, SLA thresholds, auth setup, summary |
| `scenarios/*.js` | One readable function per business scenario (edit freely; remove the first `// GENERATED` line to keep your edits when regenerating) |
| `lib/common.js` | Runtime helpers: request and step execution, correlation, think time, executors |
| `workload.json` | Workload model: arrival rates, users, SLAs, test-type load shapes. Tune numbers here without touching code |
| `data/*.csv` | Test data (one row per virtual user/iteration) |

